      "problem": "Which three numbers **CANNOT** be the measures of the sides of a triangle?",
      "worked_solution": "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nIn particular, for the numbers $8,6,14$ to represent the sides of a triangle, the number $14$ must be smaller than the sum of the numbers $8$ and $6.$ But this is not the case:\n\n$$\n8+6=14≯14\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers $8,6,14$ cannot represent the sides of a triangle.",
